  "prompt_text": "The figure is a simplified cross-section of a neutron star, showing how its composition changes from the surface inward as density rises. A very thin outer layer is labeled as a light atmosphere made of elements such as H, He, and C. Beneath that, the crust is split into an outer crust of ions and electrons and an inner crust where an ion lattice is immersed in superfluid neutrons.\n\nMost of the star’s volume is the core. The outer core is shown as a liquid containing electrons, muons, superfluid neutrons, and superconducting protons. At the very center, the inner core is highlighted as uncertain, with question marks and possible exotic matter such as hyperons or quarks, emphasizing that its state is still unknown.\n\nThe diagram also ties these layers to approximate sizes and densities. The crust is only a small fraction of the radius, with labels around 0.1 km and 0.5 km for outer regions, while the core extends to about 10 km and the full star radius appears to be roughly 12–15 km. Density markers increase inward from the neutron-drip threshold to nuclear density, then to about twice nuclear density and finally around 10^15 g cm^-3 near the center.",
